### Bug report

## Potential Problem

In /Objects/unicodeobject.c in the PyUnicode_4BYTE_KIND branch https://github.com/python/cpython/blob/3.12/Objects/unicodeobject.c#L1922. A static analyzer has identified a genuine issue: `*str` is of type `char` (which is signed on x86/x64 architectures). Consequently, when Latin-1 bytes in the range `0x80`–`0xFF` are cast to `Py_UCS4`, they undergo sign extension—for example, `0xE9` becomes `0xFFFFFFE9`. This value exceeds `MAX_UNICODE` and violates the invariants of the `str` object. This code path can be triggered via the exported function `_PyUnicodeWriter_WriteLatin1String` when using a `PyUnicode_4BYTE_KIND` buffer (while not invoked internally by CPython itself, the issue can manifest in extensions). The fix is ​​to introduce an intermediate cast to `Py_UCS1`

```
    case PyUnicode_4BYTE_KIND: {
        Py_UCS4 *start = (Py_UCS4 *)data + index;
        Py_UCS4 *ucs4 = start;

        for (; str < end; ++ucs4, ++str)
            *ucs4 = (Py_UCS4)*str;

        assert((ucs4 - start) <= PyUnicode_GET_LENGTH(unicode));
        break;
    }
    default:
        Py_UNREACHABLE();
    }
}
```

## Possible Solution

Add an intermediate cast to Py_UCS1:
`*ucs4 = (Py_UCS4)(Py_UCS1)*str;`

Found by Linux Verification Center (portal.linuxtesting.ru (https://portal.linuxtesting.ru/)) with SVACE.

Author D. Babushkin.


