### To Reproduce

A fully static route on Next.js **16.4.4** with `cacheComponents: true` that reads **server-side `searchParams`** inside a `<Suspense>` boundary never performs the streamed boundary swap after hydration: the shell hydrates and stays stuck on the streaming phase, so the preview content behind the `?state=` query never appears. Both dev and production (`next build && next start`), and both streaming phase outcomes were tried.

`next.config.ts`:

```ts
import type { NextConfig } from "next";

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  cacheComponents: true,
};

export default nextConfig;
```

`app/login/page.tsx`:

```tsx
import { Suspense } from "react";
import InvalidSlot from "./invalid-slot";

export default function Login({ searchParams }: { searchParams: Promise<{ state?: string }> }) {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Shell</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>preview placeholder</p>}>
        <InvalidSlot state={searchParams.then((sp) => sp.state)} />
      </Suspense>
    </main>
  );
}
```

Observed: the page is marked Static by `next build`, the initial HTML contains the streaming marker, the shell hydrates — and the boundary content never swaps in. The `?state=invalid` variant never renders its slot content.

Expected (per the docs' "Pass searchParams without awaiting" / suspense pattern, https://nextjs.org/docs/messages/blocking-prerender-runtime): after hydration the boundary swaps and the streamed content appears.

Workaround (what we shipped): drop the server-side read entirely and read `location.search` in a client leaf after hydration:

```tsx
"use client";
const [state, setState] = useState<string | undefined>();
useEffect(() => { setState(new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get("state") ?? undefined); }, []);
```

This keeps the route fully static

