- `next@16.2.0`+ and canary — fixed by #90158 (per the closing comment on #79182)

### Describe the bug

`NextURL` canonicalizes loopback hosts to `localhost`, which is intended. On the 15.5 line the implementation rewrites the **whole URL string** before parsing, so percent-encoded query values are rewritten as well:

```js
// next@15.5.25 — dist/server/web/next-url.js
const REGEX_LOCALHOST_HOSTNAME = /(?!^https?:\/\/)(127(?:\.(?:25[0-5]|2[0-4][0-9]|[01]?[0-9][0-9]?)){3}|\[::1\]|localhost)/;
function parseURL(url, base) {
  return new URL(
    String(url).replace(REGEX_LOCALHOST_HOSTNAME, 'localhost'),
    base && String(base).replace(REGEX_LOCALHOST_HOSTNAME, 'localhost')
  );
}
```

`request.nextUrl.searchParams.get('redirect_uri')` therefore returns `http://localhost:9931/sso/callback` for a request that carried `redirect_uri=http%3a%2f%2f127.0.0.1%3a9931%2fsso%2fcallback`.

Because `String.prototype.replace` is called without the `g` flag, only the **first** match is rewritten, so the outcome also depends on the server's own base host:

| Server started with | Value seen by `searchParams.get('redirect_uri')` |
| --- | --- |
| `HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0` (standalone `server.js` default, `next start -H 0.0.0.0`) | `http://localhost:9931/sso/callback` |
| hostname `localhost` (e.g. `next dev` defaults) | `http://127.0.0.1:9931/sso/callback` (query survives because the base host consumes the single replacement) |

This makes the same request behave differently depending on how the server was started, which is what makes it hard to diagnose in self-hosted deployments.

